@how own mechanism: When a finite family of automorphisms fixes a candidate intermediate field and direct membership in that field is difficult, compare the extension degree with the number of automorphisms and turn the comparison into a Galois fixed-field theorem. (source: this pattern's ! conclusion line; L8 rationale-backfill, zai-1, 2026-09-05)

! conclusion:
  When a finite family of automorphisms fixes a candidate intermediate field
  and direct membership in that field is difficult, compare the extension
  degree with the number of automorphisms and turn the comparison into a
  Galois fixed-field theorem.

  + IF: You have proved that every automorphism in an explicit finite family
    fixes an intermediate field K pointwise, and need to show that every
    element fixed by the family lies in K.

  + HOWEVER: The easy inclusion from K into the fixed elements does not
    supply the reverse inclusion, and a direct normal-form proof for fixed
    elements may be unavailable.

  + THEN: Repackage the family as K-algebra automorphisms, prove that its
    parameterization is bijective onto the full automorphism type, and compute
    Module.finrank K L.  If Nat.card (L ≃ₐ[K] L) equals that finrank, use
    IsGalois.of_card_aut_eq_finrank, put a fixed element in the fixed field of
    the top automorphism subgroup via IntermediateField.mem_fixedField_iff,
    and rewrite that field with IsGalois.fixedField_top.
    + LEAN:
      api[IsGalois.of_card_aut_eq_finrank]
      api[IntermediateField.mem_fixedField_iff]
      api[IsGalois.fixedField_top]
      api[AlgEquiv.card_le]

  + BECAUSE: Equality in the degree-versus-automorphism bound certifies that
    the extension is Galois; the general Galois fixed-field theorem then
    supplies the hard reverse inclusion without classifying arbitrary fixed
    rational expressions.
